A||||5||deceler8||Car won't crank, but everything lights up...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||09-08-2001||10:22 AM||||When I turn the key to the crank position, I get nothing, even though the gauges react and I can hear the fuel pump.<P>This used to be a problem if I tried to start the car with the tilt wheel in the "up" position, then I discovered that the car would crank fine with the wheel in the "down" position.<P>But even now with the wheel in the down position, I get nothing.<P>Any ideas on why this is happening and how it can be fixed ? And if this is difficult to fix, is it time for the "engine start" button ?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero_hunter85||09-08-2001||11:10 AM||||its probably a short in the wires inside the steering coloum. if you dont want to take apart the coloum then you might want to get a used one and install the whole thing.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||09-08-2001||11:18 AM||||first I would adjust the ignition switch (dont confuse this with the ignition lock cylender) it is near the bottom of the steering column and mounted on the top side of it.  Two screws hold it in place and it is adjustable.  No ignition wires go down column to be broken or shorted when tilting column only wiper/cruise/signal wires. Between ign cyl and ign switch is a rod.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero_hunter85||09-08-2001||11:19 AM||||ALSO<BR>the ignition switch mounted on the coloum with the wires pluged could be out of adjustment or worn out. also check to make sure the rod from the key cylinder to the switch is working.   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-08-2001||02:07 PM||||It could be as simple as a small adjustment of the ignition switch. The switch has a nasty habbit of working loose over time. It doen't take much to work out of position on the column.<P>Lamp test is actually a seperate key position in between run and start. Lamp Test usually gets ignored because it's hard for most people to notice the diference between it and full start position. I even ignore it in my articles for that reason but it is still a seperate position.<P>You'll need to drop the column to get at the switch. There's a whole raft of notes on the column in my cave along with a link to Oliver's illustraded tear apart.<P>Also read the wire service article in my cave. You could have crud in c500 or a loose wire at the starter.||||reg||1
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||09-08-2001||02:11 PM||||I agree that it might be the switch too. But don't over look the starter . ||||reg||1
